{"id":"sw-a93fd7","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-641d82dc-482b-420f-aed1-321165ac3bc6","text":"## Summary\n\nApplied the 2026-08-19 internal review to `book/chapters/05-input-and-platform-interaction.qmd`, with spec sync in `docs/architecture/describing-a-game.md` §5 and an em-dash fix in `book/figures/fixed-tick-edge-problem.tex`.\n\n- **1.1** `push_map`/`pop_map` moved to `GameContext` in `@lst-action-queries`, `@lst-game-context`, and the spec (both the Input section and a pointer comment in the GameContext block).\n- **1.2** Consumed-once latch replaced by **tick-stamped edges** (Godot precedent, verified): edges stamped during INPUT with the next fixed tick's index; `fixed_action_pressed` true only in the matching tick. Query is pure, so the chapter's no-side-effect promise now holds, and multiple consumers work. Section retitled \"Tick-Stamped Edges and the Intent Pattern\" with the three boundary conditions (context stack at stamp time, resume behavior, sub-frame collapse).\n- **1.5** New `Binding::ControllerStick { device, stick, dead_zone }` with **radial** dead zone; `ControllerAxis` stays scalar. \"move\" example rebound. Radial-vs-axial explained with UE (verified: `UInputModifierDeadZone` Radial/Axial), Unity (`StickDeadzone`/`AxisDeadzone`), Godot (`get_vector`).\n- **1.3/1.4** Controllers are `Vec\u003cOption\u003cControllerState\u003e\u003e` connection slots (holes keep indices stable; no steady-state allocation); quarantine listing gains `CursorMoved` + connect/disconnect arms; action edges computed at the action layer by diffing resolved values (covers controller buttons without device edge state).\n- **1.6** Axis resolution: largest magnitude that frame (Unity-style disambiguation) replaces the two-rule wording.\n- **Facts**: Sekiro carries the Xbox-prompts story; Cyberpunk claim dropped; `UPlayerInput` replaces \"`FInputState`\"; USB-IF vendor-ID scoping; CVAA scoped to communication features; composites \"clamped\" not \"normalized\".\n- **Citations** (existing bib only): `gregory2026game`, `gaffer-fix-timestep`, `ford-gdc2017`.\n- **Trims**: DeviceFilter rationale, replay quadruplication, drift re-derivation, console-port duplicate; plus typos, missing quote, spaced hyphens, figure `---`, wrong `@sec` ref, stale comments, App-trait signature alignment.\n\n## Rationale\n\nBoth redesigns follow the one engine that ships each mechanism natively (Godot's clock-aware edges; UE/Unity/Godot's radial stick dead zones), per the \"proven techniques first\" directive.\n\n## Decisions made (not in the user's explicit list)\n\n- `Vec\u003cOption\u003cControllerState\u003e\u003e` slot semantics in chapter **and spec**: the only design where the stable-index claim, the quarantine listing, and the no-per-frame-allocation rule all hold. Revisit if identity-only addressing is preferred.\n- Largest-magnitude axis resolution in chapter **and spec**.\n\n## Status\n\nChapter renders clean via Quarto (HTML smoke test; zero warnings, citations resolve). All listing/figure cross-references verified. **Note:** raw word count rose 12,865 → 14,017 (~12k prose vs. 8-10k target): requested additions outweighed the ~700 words of trims. A dedicated slimming pass is possible if wanted. No commit; awaiting user review."},"created_at":"2026-08-19T12:55:54.078157Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.235 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-a93fd7","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"updated"},"created_at":"2026-08-19T13:02:59.184597Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.235 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-a93fd7","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-c479b028-5916-45fb-8ddd-99922ce94824","text":"## Review round 2 applied\n\n**User feedback:** when engines offer specific implementations/guidance, always discuss and compare the options; when Smallworld follows one, explicitly argue why it wins on the merits against each alternative. \"Didn't research the others\" / \"first one that looked good\" are never acceptable — Smallworld cherry-picks the absolute best ideas.\n\n**Changes:**\n- Spec updated first (new \"Review round 2\" section) per workflow.\n- **Tick-Stamped Edges section expanded** from \"most engines leave it to game code + Godot\" to the three real strategies, each verified against vendor docs: Unity's global update-mode re-clock (incl. its changelog recording both failure cases as bugs in fixed mode), Unreal's structural avoidance + `UAsyncPhysicsInputComponent` marshaling for the UE5 async physics tick, Godot's clock-aware stamped queries.\n- **Explicit winner-rationale paragraph** added, arguing per alternative: Unity's global re-clock breaks one consumer to fix the other under Smallworld's dual-clock phase model; UE's marshaling buys thread-boundary/network-prediction machinery Smallworld's single-threaded tick doesn't need (noted as the escape hatch if fixed ticks ever move off-thread); Godot's is the only strategy correct in both loops with no mode and no data movement, at two integers per action. Also clarified Smallworld needs only the fixed-tick half of Godot's dual stamp (frame edges come from the action-layer diff).\n- **Rule made durable**: added to `.claude/skills/book/SKILL.md` (\"Compare engine-offered solutions, then argue the winner on merits\") so future chapter passes inherit it.\n\n**Status:** HTML render clean, zero dash violations, word count 14,463 (+446 for the comparison; over-target accepted per user in favor of comparison depth). Still awaiting tophat — no walkthrough/commit."},"created_at":"2026-08-19T13:04:20.675153Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.235 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-a93fd7","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-76d82a54-639f-4306-8713-1388eef19b87","text":"## Round 3: networking forward-thread\n\nPer user direction after the \"would networking force UE5's solution?\" discussion:\n\n- **Ch05** winner-rationale extended with an explicit escape-hatch paragraph: adopting Godot's strategy is \"a refusal to pay for marshaling before anything needs it\"; the two future drivers (off-thread fixed ticks, networked prediction/rollback) are named; the per-tick input record (`TickInput`) is shown to fall mechanically out of the tick stamps; the query API survives with a swapped backing store; forward pointers to Ch21 (prediction makes the record concrete) and Ch22 (same record as the unit of replay).\n- **Ch21 stub** now carries the thread: materialize `TickInput` from the stamps (adopting the *data shape* of `UAsyncPhysicsInputComponent` without its API), showcase the backing-store swap, and note rollback re-simulates ticks (query purity is what makes multi-run safe). \n- **Ch22 stub**: recording format corrected from `(frame_index, Input)` to `(frame_index, game_delta, Input)` — **determinism bug found in the stub**: without the per-frame delta, replay cannot reproduce the accumulator's tick cadence, so tick stamps and every `fixed_action_pressed` result could diverge. Also added the backing-store-swap showcase bullet to the rollback section (payoff of Ch05's escape hatch).\n\nCh05 re-renders clean, zero dash violations. Still awaiting tophat."},"created_at":"2026-08-19T13:11:01.648317Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.235 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-438537","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Ch01-04 engine-comparison audit + Tier-1 retrofit","description":"Apply the tiered engine-comparison policy (codified 2026-08-19 in `.claude/skills/book/SKILL.md` and first applied in ch05's Tick-Stamped Edges section, sw-a93fd7) retroactively to chapters 01-04.\n\n## Phase 1: Audit (cheap, do first)\n\nSweep chapters 01-04 and enumerate every **Tier-1 passage**: a design point where the major engines (UE, Unity, Godot at minimum) ship *divergent* implementations or guidance and Smallworld picks one or invents its own. For each fork, record: chapter/section, the decision, one line per engine on what it actually ships (flag which claims need vendor-doc verification), and whether the current prose already carries any comparison. Deliver the list to `book/reviews/` with a per-fork effort estimate before starting Phase 2.\n\nExpected shape: ch01-02 are historical surveys (near zero forks); the real candidates are in ch03-04, e.g. the dedicated render thread + message passing (vs. Unity jobs-on-main-thread, UE parallel-rendering task graph), interpolation vs. extrapolation, bounded-channel backpressure, teardown/control-channel formalization. Rough prior: 8-12 forks total.\n\n## Phase 2: Retrofit (per chapter, ride along with revision passes)\n\nFor each audited fork, expand the passage from problem/solution/Smallworld-implementation to problem/options/comparison/Smallworld-pick/rationale:\n\n- Verify each engine's approach against vendor docs (web research), never from memory.\n- Argue the winner per-alternative on merits in Smallworld's context; ch05 Tick-Stamped Edges is the ceiling, a mainstream-following fork needs roughly half that depth.\n- **Tier-2 (consensus) and Tier-3 (micro-decision) passages are explicitly out of scope**; do not force per-alternative rationale onto settled questions.\n- Each expansion adds 200-400 words; hunt for redundancy to trim in exchange. Word target is a soft signal per the updated skill; if a chapter comes out lean and still far over, raise a chapter-split proposal instead of trimming depth.\n\nPrefer executing Phase 2 chapter-by-chapter alongside each chapter's next scheduled revision pass rather than as a standalone campaign.","status":"PLANNED","estimate":5,"sort_order":"a0","dependencies":[{"source_id":"sw-438537","target_id":"sw-a93fd7","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["book","revision","audit"]},"created_at":"2026-08-19T13:25:04.671393Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.235 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-a93fd7","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-7609657b-3096-43f1-adaf-11d52fd55896","text":"## Round 4: policy codified, retrofit filed\n\nUser ratified the tiered comparison policy and demoted the per-chapter word target from hard cap to soft signal.\n\n- `.claude/skills/book/SKILL.md`: the comparison bullet now scales by engine divergence (Tier 1 full treatment with ch05 Tick-Stamped Edges as the ceiling; Tier 2 consensus = historical grounding only; Tier 3 micro-decisions = local rationale). \"Target length\" rewritten: 8-10k words is a reference point to flag runaway writing; never cut comparison depth to hit it; large overshoot on a lean chapter signals a chapter split, raised with the author rather than trimmed silently.\n- Follow-up filed: **sw-438537** (PLANNED, relates_to this issue) — Phase 1 audit enumerating ch01-04 Tier-1 forks with per-fork estimates delivered to `book/reviews/`, Phase 2 retrofit riding along each chapter's next revision pass. Tier-2/3 passages explicitly out of scope.\n\nThis issue's chapter/spec/figure work is unchanged since round 3; still awaiting tophat."},"created_at":"2026-08-19T13:25:17.30734Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.235 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
